From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Doors <doors@localhost>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] riscv: dts: rm69a10: expose the BOOT button as a gpio-keys
 KEY_BACK

Applies on top of the LILYGO BSP stack (after 0064) and Doors 0070-0073.
Doors-owned; see docs/decisions/ADR-011-kernel-patches.md and
docs/hardware/K230_BUTTONS.md in the Doors repository.

SW3 "boot" is the BOOT0 strap: pad IO0 (GPIO0), R8 10k pull-up to VDD_1V8,
the switch to GND, so active low (schematic sheet 2/6, DOCUMENTED; levels
VERIFIED on unit B). The boot firmware leaves the pad as function BOOT0
with its input disabled (iomux word 0xAC4 on both units), so no GPIO
consumer can see the button, and the tree has no pin group for IO0. Only
the device tree can hand the pad to a driver; the alternative is a
userspace /dev/mem write, which Doors does not do.

Added: a pin group putting IO0 into its GPIO0 function with the input on,
the output off and the pull-up on (the driver keeps the pad's drive
strength and voltage bits, giving 0x344, the value the vendor launcher
wrote and the one the unit B press test used), and a gpio-keys node on
gpio0 line 0, active low, reporting KEY_BACK. gpio-keys is built in
(CONFIG_KEYBOARD_GPIO=y). Nothing else in the tree changes: the decompiled
DTB gains exactly these two nodes.

The strap itself is unchanged and cannot be changed here: the boot ROM
samples BOOT0 at reset, so holding the button during reset or power-on
still selects eMMC boot. RESET, the PMU power key and U-Boot are not
touched. The HDMI tree (k230-canmv-rm69a10-hdmi.dts) is not changed.

Signed-off-by: Doors <doors@localhost>
---
 arch/riscv/boot/dts/canaan/k230-canmv-rm69a10.dts | 35 +++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 35 insertions(+)

--- a/arch/riscv/boot/dts/canaan/k230-canmv-rm69a10.dts
+++ b/arch/riscv/boot/dts/canaan/k230-canmv-rm69a10.dts
@@ -1,6 +1,7 @@
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0 OR MIT
 #include "k230.dtsi"
 #include "display-rm69a10-568x1232.dtsi"
+#include <dt-bindings/input/input.h>
 / {
 	model = "Canaan CanMV-K230 with RM69A10 OLED";
 	compatible = "canaan,canmv-k230", "canaan,kendryte-k230";
@@ -38,6 +39,26 @@
 		canaan,k230-audio-codec     = <&inno_codec>;
 		canaan,external-i2s-output-default;
 	};
+
+	/*
+	 * SW3 "boot": the BOOT0 strap on pad IO0, R8 10k pull-up, the switch
+	 * to GND (active low). After the boot ROM has sampled the strap it is
+	 * an ordinary input; Doors reads it as a navigation key. Holding it
+	 * during reset or power-on still selects the wrong boot medium.
+	 */
+	gpio-keys {
+		compatible = "gpio-keys";
+		label = "K230 BOOT Key";
+		pinctrl-names = "default";
+		pinctrl-0 = <&boot_key_pins>;
+
+		key-boot {
+			label = "BOOT";
+			gpios = <&gpio0_ports 0 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+			linux,code = <KEY_BACK>;
+			debounce-interval = <20>;
+		};
+	};
 };
 &uart0 { status = "okay"; };
 &uart3 {
@@ -204,6 +225,20 @@
 		output-enable;
 		drive-strength = <8>;
 	};
+
+	/*
+	 * IO0 is left by the boot firmware as BOOT0 with its input off
+	 * (0xAC4), where no GPIO can see it. GPIO0 function, input on,
+	 * output off, pull-up: 0x344 with the pad's existing drive strength
+	 * and voltage bits kept.
+	 */
+	boot_key_pins: boot_key_pins {
+		pins = K230_IO0;
+		function = K230_IO0_GPIO0;
+		input-enable;
+		output-disable;
+		bias-pull-up;
+	};
 };
 
 &uart1 {
